{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-156052",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{300}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${300}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275599/assets/731a06693d32fde4db47283c1fd31e80891c9b4e05e4917b809379d442f410d0.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={300}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{300}^{∘} \\\\  & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{300}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{300}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{300}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
